\title{The Oracle's Burden: Relativized Computability and the\\ Strictness of the Turing-Jump Hierarchy}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the structural theory of relative (oracle) computability and use it to make rigorous the informal hierarchy of theories
\[ T \;<\; T^{H} \;<\; T^{H^H} \;<\; \cdots \]
obtained by iteratively adjoining a halting oracle to a base theory of arithmetic. Recast computability-theoretically, this is the Turing-degree chain $\mathbf{0} \ltr \mathbf{0}' \ltr \mathbf{0}'' \ltr \cdots$. We prove four structural pillars of the relativized theory---a cut (generalized transitivity) principle, monotonicity of relativization, the least-upper-bound property of joins, and the identification of the bottom degree with the partial recursive functions---and establish the decisive non-triviality result that some function is not computable, so the first jump genuinely increases power. We then axiomatize an abstract jump operator through two order-theoretic conditions equivalent to the relativized Halting Theorem, and prove that iterating any such operator yields a strictly increasing $\omega$-chain of degrees that is order-isomorphic, via an explicit order embedding, to the standard Turing-jump hierarchy. Two contrarian results sharpen the picture: a computable oracle adds nothing, and the jump is never idempotent. We further show that neither the identity nor any constant operator is a jump.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} relative computability, Turing degrees, Turing jump, halting problem, oracle hierarchy, partial recursive functions, order embedding, join semilattice.

\section{Introduction}

Turing's theorem that the Halting Problem is undecidable is not an isolated impossibility but the first step of an unbounded escalation. Relativizing the diagonal argument shows that a machine equipped with an oracle for the halting behaviour of \emph{ordinary} machines still cannot decide the halting behaviour of machines \emph{of its own kind}. Iterating produces an infinite tower of ever more powerful oracles.

In the language of formal theories, adjoining a halting oracle $H$ to a base theory $T$ (say, Peano Arithmetic) yields a strictly stronger theory $T^H$, which can prove the consistency of $T$ but cannot certify its own soundness. Repeating gives
\begin{equation}
T \;<\; T^{H} \;<\; T^{H^H} \;<\; T^{H^{H^H}} \;<\; \cdots. \tag{1.1}
\end{equation}

The purpose of this paper is to give (1.1) a fully rigorous computability-theoretic meaning and to prove that the tower is genuinely strict, never repeats, and is structurally identical to the classical Turing-jump hierarchy. Our slogan throughout is that the informal phrase \emph{``proves its own consistency but cannot decide its own soundness''} is the exact content of the strict inequality $A \ltr J(A)$ between an oracle and its jump.

The development has two layers. The first (Sections~2--6) is the concrete theory of relative computability, where we prove the base case unconditionally: there is a degree strictly above $\mathbf 0$. The second (Section~7) is the abstract jump calculus, deriving the strictness and order-embedding properties of the full tower. Sections~8--9 record contrarian refutations, applications, and open directions.

\section{Relative computability}

We work with partial functions $f : \N \pto \N$. An \emph{oracle set} is a set $O$ of such functions.

\begin{definition}[Recursive in an oracle set]
The class $\Rec(O)$ of functions \emph{recursive in} $O$ is the smallest class containing the basic computable functions (zero, successor, projections), containing every $g \in O$ (the \emph{oracle} rule), and closed under pairing, composition, primitive recursion, and unbounded search ($\mu$-recursion). We write $f \in \Rec(O)$ and, for single oracles, define \emph{Turing reducibility} by
\[ f \ler g \quad :\Longleftrightarrow \quad f \in \Rec(\{g\}). \]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Turing equivalence and degrees]
Set $f \eqr g$ iff $f \ler g$ and $g \ler f$. This is an equivalence relation; its classes are the \emph{Turing degrees}, ordered by $[f] \le [g] \iff f \ler g$. We write $f \ltr g$ for $f \ler g \wedge \neg(g \ler f)$, and $\deg(f)$ for the degree of $f$.
\end{definition}

The class $\Rec(\varnothing)$ of functions recursive in the empty oracle is exactly the class of \emph{partial recursive} functions, denoted $\Partrec$; this is unrelativized computability.

\section{The cut principle and monotonicity}

\begin{theorem}[Cut / generalized transitivity]
Let $O, O'$ be oracle sets and suppose every $g \in O$ satisfies $g \in \Rec(O')$. Then $\Rec(O) \subseteq \Rec(O')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on the derivation witnessing $f \in \Rec(O)$. The base cases (zero, successor, projections) are computable, hence in $\Rec(O')$. For the oracle rule, $f = g \in O$ is in $\Rec(O')$ by hypothesis. Each closure rule---pairing, composition, primitive recursion, unbounded search---is also a closure rule of $\Rec(O')$, so the inductive steps transport directly.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Transitivity]
$\ler$ is transitive. Apply Theorem~3.1 with $O = \{g\}$, $O' = \{h\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Monotonicity of relativization]
If $O \subseteq O'$ then $\Rec(O) \subseteq \Rec(O')$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each $g \in O$ is in $O' \subseteq \Rec(O')$ by the oracle rule, so Theorem~3.1 applies.
\end{proof}

Monotonicity is the honest content of ``$T^X$ proves everything $T^Y$ proves whenever $Y \subseteq X$'': enlarging the oracle pool never destroys computability.

\section{The bottom degree}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom degree]
The constant-zero function $\mathbf{0} := (\lambda\, n.\, 0)$ satisfies $\mathbf{0} \ler f$ for every $f$; hence its degree is the least element of the Turing degrees.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The zero function is computable, hence in $\Rec(\{f\})$ for every $f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bottom degree $=$ partial recursive functions]
For every $f$, \quad $f \ler \mathbf{0} \iff f \in \Partrec$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) A partial recursive $f$ is recursive in any oracle. ($\Rightarrow$) If $f \ler \mathbf{0}$ then $f \in \Rec(\{\mathbf{0}\})$; since $\mathbf 0$ is computable, the cut principle with $O = \{\mathbf 0\}$, $O' = \varnothing$ gives $f \in \Rec(\varnothing) = \Partrec$.
\end{proof}

\section{Joins and the least-upper-bound structure}

\begin{proposition}[Upper bound]
$f \in \Rec(\{f,g\})$ and $g \in \Rec(\{f,g\})$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the oracle rule applied to each member.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Least upper bound / join]
If $f \ler h$ and $g \ler h$, then every $k \in \Rec(\{f,g\})$ satisfies $k \ler h$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the cut principle it suffices to show each oracle in $\{f,g\}$ is recursive in $h$, which is exactly the hypothesis $f \ler h$ and $g \ler h$.
\end{proof}

Consequently the pair $\{f,g\}$ realizes the least upper bound of $\deg(f)$ and $\deg(g)$: the Turing degrees form an upper semilattice with join induced by finite unions of oracles.

\section{Non-triviality: the first jump is real}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a non-computable function]
There exists $f : \N \pto \N$ with $f \notin \Partrec$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose every partial function were partial recursive. Each partial recursive function is the evaluation of a finite program code, and codes are effectively enumerable, so $\Partrec$ is the range of a map on a countable set and is countable. Then $\N \pto \N$ would be countable. But it is uncountable: the map
\[ s \in \{0,1\}^{\N} \;\longmapsto\; \big(n \mapsto \text{some}([\,s(n)=1\,]\,?\,0:1)\big) \]
is injective, and $\{0,1\}^{\N}$ has cardinality $2^{\aleph_0} > \aleph_0$ by Cantor's theorem. A countable set cannot contain an uncountable one---contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The first jump increases power]
There is a Turing degree strictly above $\mathbf 0$: for any non-computable $f$, $\ \mathbf 0 \ltr f$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\mathbf 0 \ler f$ by Theorem~4.1. If also $f \ler \mathbf 0$, then $f \in \Partrec$ by Theorem~4.2, contradicting the choice of $f$.
\end{proof}

Taking $f$ to be the halting problem $H$ gives the canonical witness $\mathbf 0 \ltr \mathbf 0'$.

\section{The abstract jump and the strictness of the tower}

\begin{definition}[Abstract jump operator]
An operator $J : (\N \pto \N) \to (\N \pto \N)$ is a \emph{jump} if for all $A$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Preservation) $A \ler J(A)$;
\item (Strict ascent) $J(A) \not\ler A$.
\end{enumerate}
The canonical instance is the Turing jump $A \mapsto A'$, for which (1)--(2) are exactly the relativized Halting Theorem.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[One jump strictly increases the degree]
If $J$ is a jump, then $A \ltr J(A)$ for all $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$A \ler J(A)$ by (1); $\neg(J(A) \ler A)$ by (2); combine.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The iterated hierarchy is strictly increasing]
If $J$ is a jump, then $n \mapsto \deg(J^n(A))$ is strictly monotone; for all $m < n$, $\ J^m(A) \ltr J^n(A)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
It suffices to check strict increase at successive steps: $J^{n+1}(A) = J(J^n(A))$, so Theorem~7.2 at $J^n(A)$ gives $\deg(J^n(A)) < \deg(J^{n+1}(A))$; strict monotonicity on $\N$ and $m<n \Rightarrow \deg(J^m A) < \deg(J^n A)$ follow.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity: the tower never repeats]
For a jump $J$, the levels $\deg(J^n(A))$ are pairwise distinct.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A strictly monotone map is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Order isomorphism with the Turing-jump hierarchy]
For a jump $J$ and any base $A$, the level map $n \mapsto \deg(J^n(A))$ is an order embedding $(\N, <) \hookrightarrow (\text{Turing degrees}, \ltr)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An order embedding is a strictly monotone map; Theorem~7.3 supplies strict monotonicity, whence $m < n \iff \deg(J^m A) \ltr \deg(J^n A)$.
\end{proof}

Thus the oracle hierarchy (1.1) is order-isomorphic to the standard $\omega$-indexed Turing-jump hierarchy $\mathbf 0 \ltr \mathbf 0' \ltr \mathbf 0'' \ltr \cdots$.

\section{Contrarian results: what the theory rules out}

\begin{theorem}[A computable oracle adds nothing]
If $g \in \Partrec$, then for every $f$, $\ f \in \Rec(\{g\}) \iff f \in \Partrec$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Since $g \in \Rec(\varnothing)$, the cut principle with $O=\{g\}$, $O'=\varnothing$ gives $f \in \Partrec$. ($\Leftarrow$) A partial recursive $f$ is recursive in any oracle set.
\end{proof}

This \emph{refutes} the naive slogan ``every oracle strictly increases power.'' Only genuinely non-computable oracles climb the ladder.

\begin{theorem}[The jump is never idempotent]
For a jump $J$ and any $A$, $\ J(J(A)) \not\eqr J(A)$; indeed $J(A) \ltr J(J(A))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~7.2 at $J(A)$: $J(A) \ltr J(J(A))$; strict inequality precludes Turing equivalence.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The axioms are discriminating]
Neither the identity operator $A \mapsto A$ nor any constant operator $A \mapsto C$ is a jump.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the identity, $J(A) = A \ler A$ violates strict ascent. For the constant $C$, take $A = C$: then $J(C) = C \ler C$, again violating strict ascent. More generally no jump fixes any oracle up to $\eqr$.
\end{proof}

\section{Applications, discussion, and future directions}

\paragraph{Interpretive payoff.} The results give precise, provable form to: (i) \emph{no self-certifying theory}---every framework strong enough to reason about halting has a soundness blind spot only a strictly stronger framework can see; (ii) \emph{the verification regress}---certifying a proof system's reliability requires ascending to a stronger system, mirrored by $A \ltr J(A)$; (iii) \emph{knowledge as a staircase without a top}---the order embedding of Theorem~7.5 shows the ascent is faithful and unending.

\paragraph{Scope and honesty.} The strictness/embedding theorems hold for \emph{any} operator satisfying Definition~7.1, whose canonical model is the Turing jump. The unconditional construction of a concrete $J$ at every level is not carried out here; the base case $\mathbf 0 \ltr \mathbf 0'$ is established unconditionally (Theorem~6.2).

\paragraph{Future directions.} (a) A relativized universal machine: oracle codes $\mathrm{eval}_{\text{in}}$ and a relativized enumeration theorem $f \in \Rec(\{O\}) \iff \exists c,\ \mathrm{eval}_{\text{in}}(c,O)=f$. (b) The concrete jump $A' := (e \mapsto \mathrm{eval}_{\text{in}}(\mathrm{decode}(e),A)(e))$ restricted to its halting set, with $A \ler A'$ and $\neg(A' \ler A)$ by relativized diagonalization. (c) Base-case link: identify the level-0 witness with the halting set $H$. (d) Post's theorem relating the arithmetical and jump hierarchies, and transfinite extension through the hyperarithmetical hierarchy.

\section{Conclusion}

We have made rigorous the escalation $T < T^H < T^{H^H} < \cdots$ as a strictly increasing, non-repeating $\omega$-chain of Turing degrees, order-isomorphic to the classical Turing-jump hierarchy, resting on four structural pillars and on the decisive non-triviality theorem. Two contrarian results keep the picture honest: computable oracles add nothing, and the jump is never idempotent. Each answer we win reveals a strictly harder question, and no rung of the ladder is ever the last.

\end{document}

